# Flight instruments, attitude instrument flying and IFR aircraft systems: Instrument Flying Handbook
# FAA-H-8083-15B ch. 5 to 7, AIM 7-2 (altimeter errors), AC 91-74 (icing systems). ACS II.A, II.B, IV, VII.D.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 1, "Primary instruments", "In straight-and-level flight, which is the primary instrument for pitch?",
      ["Altimeter", "Attitude indicator", "Vertical speed indicator"],
      "The Instrument Flying Handbook names the altimeter as primary for pitch when holding an altitude. The attitude indicator is the control instrument and supports it."),
    S(1, 1, "Primary instruments", "In straight-and-level flight, which is the primary instrument for bank?",
      ["Heading indicator", "Turn coordinator", "Attitude indicator"],
      "While holding a heading, the heading indicator is primary for bank because any bank shows up at once as a heading change."),
    S(1, 2, "Scanning errors", "A pilot stares at the altimeter while the heading drifts 20 degrees. Which scan error is that?",
      ["Fixation", "Omission", "Emphasis"],
      "Fixation is staring at one instrument and neglecting the rest. Omission leaves an instrument out of the cross-check; emphasis relies too heavily on one easy instrument."),
    S(1, 2, "Unusual attitudes", "Nose high, airspeed falling. What is the recovery order?",
      ["Add power, lower the nose, level the wings", "Level the wings, add power, raise the nose", "Reduce power, lower the nose, level the wings"],
      "For a nose-high attitude with decreasing airspeed, the Instrument Flying Handbook gives: increase power, lower the nose to prevent a stall, then level the wings, almost at once."),
    S(1, 3, "Static blockage", "The static port ices over and you descend. How does the airspeed indicator read?",
      ["Too high", "Too low", "Correctly"],
      "With the static pressure trapped at the higher altitude, the difference between pitot and static pressure grows as you descend, so indicated airspeed reads higher than actual."),
    S(1, 2, "Vacuum failure", "The vacuum pump fails. Which group of instruments can you still trust?",
      ["Turn coordinator, airspeed, altimeter", "Attitude and heading indicators, airspeed", "Heading indicator and VSI only"],
      "In most light airplanes the attitude and heading indicators are vacuum driven. The electric turn coordinator and the pitot-static instruments remain for partial-panel flight."),
    S(1, 3, "Altimeter errors", "Flying in air much colder than standard with the correct altimeter setting, your true altitude is:",
      ["Lower than indicated", "Higher than indicated", "Equal to indicated"],
      "Cold air is denser, so pressure levels sit lower. The altimeter overreads, putting the airplane lower than it shows; that is why cold-temperature corrections exist."),
    S(1, 2, "Glass cockpit failures", "On a glass panel, the air data computer fails. Which indications are lost?",
      ["Airspeed, altitude and vertical speed", "Attitude, heading and turn rate", "GPS course, distance and groundspeed"],
      "The ADC processes pitot-static data, so its failure removes airspeed, altitude and vertical speed. The AHRS supplies attitude and heading."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Primary instruments", "In a constant-airspeed climb, which instrument is primary for pitch?",
      ["Airspeed indicator", "Altimeter", "Vertical speed indicator"],
      "Once a constant-airspeed climb is set, the airspeed indicator is primary for pitch: a pitch error shows up first as an airspeed change."),
    S(2, 2, "Control and performance", "In the control and performance method, which are the control instruments?",
      ["Attitude and power indicators", "Altimeter and airspeed indicator", "Heading indicator and VSI"],
      "Control instruments show attitude and power directly and are set first. Performance instruments, such as the altimeter, airspeed and heading, show the result."),
    S(2, 3, "Altitude corrections", "You are 200 feet below your assigned altitude. By the handbook rule of thumb, what climb rate should you use?",
      ["400 fpm", "600 fpm", "1,000 fpm"],
      "The Instrument Flying Handbook suggests a vertical speed about double the altitude deviation: 2 x 200 feet = 400 fpm, but never more than the airplane's best rate."),
    S(2, 2, "Unusual attitudes", "Nose low, airspeed rising. What is the first step?",
      ["Reduce power", "Raise the nose", "Add full power"],
      "For a nose-low attitude with increasing airspeed, reduce power first, then level the wings with coordinated controls, then raise the nose smoothly. Pulling first in a bank tightens the spiral."),
    S(2, 2, "Scanning errors", "After rolling out of a steep turn, a pilot forgets to check heading. Which scan error is that?",
      ["Omission", "Fixation", "Emphasis"],
      "Leaving an instrument out of the cross-check is omission. The handbook's example is a roll-out where the pilot checks only the attitude indicator and not the heading indicator."),
    S(2, 3, "Alternate static", "When you open the alternate static source in the cabin, the VSI briefly shows:",
      ["A climb", "A descent", "No change"],
      "Cabin pressure is usually lower than outside static pressure, so the VSI shows a momentary climb, and the altimeter and airspeed read somewhat high."),
    S(2, 2, "Glass cockpit failures", "The AHRS fails on a glass panel. What should you fly by?",
      ["The standby attitude indicator", "The magnetic compass alone", "The moving map and synthetic vision"],
      "The AHRS supplies attitude and heading to the PFD. When it fails, fly the backup attitude instrument and use the compass for heading."),
    S(2, 2, "Altimeter setting", "Why get the local altimeter setting before an approach?",
      ["Minimums assume the current setting", "It recalibrates the VSI for descent", "It corrects the compass card for the turn"],
      "Approach altitudes are MSL and assume the current local altimeter setting. An old setting from a higher-pressure area puts you lower than indicated."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Pitot heat", "When should you turn on pitot heat?",
      ["Before visible moisture near freezing", "Only after the airspeed reads zero", "Only during the takeoff roll and climb"],
      "Pitot heat prevents ice from blocking the pitot tube. Turn it on before flying into visible moisture at or near freezing, not after the airspeed fails."),
    S(0, 2, "Ice protection", "What is the difference between anti-ice and deice equipment?",
      ["Anti-ice prevents ice; deice removes it", "Anti-ice removes ice; deice prevents it", "Both only work on the windshield"],
      "Anti-icing systems, such as heated surfaces and fluid, keep ice from forming. Deicing systems, such as pneumatic boots, remove ice after it builds."),
    S(0, 2, "Ice protection", "Pneumatic deice boots shed ice by:",
      ["Inflating to crack it off", "Heating the leading edge", "Spraying a glycol-based fluid"],
      "Boots inflate briefly, changing the leading edge shape so accreted ice cracks and is carried away by the airflow. Heat and fluid are anti-ice methods."),
    S(0, 2, "Rate instruments", "What can a turn coordinator show that a turn-and-slip indicator can't?",
      ["Rate of roll", "Bank angle in degrees", "Pitch attitude"],
      "The turn coordinator's gyro is canted, so it senses roll rate as well as yaw. A turn-and-slip indicator shows rate of turn only."),
    S(0, 1, "Standard-rate turn", "How long does a standard-rate 360-degree turn take?",
      ["2 minutes", "4 minutes", "3 minutes"],
      "A standard-rate turn is 3 degrees per second: 360 / 3 = 120 seconds, or 2 minutes."),
    S(0, 3, "Standard-rate turn", "Using 15 percent of true airspeed, about what bank gives a standard-rate turn at 120 knots?",
      ["18 degrees", "12 degrees", "15 degrees"],
      "The handbook rule of thumb is 15 percent of TAS: 0.15 x 120 = 18 degrees, or 120 / 10 = 12 plus half of that, 6, for 18."),
    S(0, 2, "Scanning errors", "A pilot relies on the VSI alone to hold altitude. Which scan error is that?",
      ["Emphasis", "Omission", "Fixation"],
      "Leaning on one instrument that seems easy to read, instead of the combination needed, is emphasis. The VSI lags and can't hold altitude by itself."),
    S(0, 3, "Unusual attitudes", "On partial panel, how can you tell that pitch is level during a recovery?",
      ["Airspeed and altimeter stop moving", "The heading stops changing", "The ball centers in the inclinometer"],
      "With the attitude indicator out, level pitch shows when the airspeed indicator and altimeter needles stop and reverse. The turn coordinator and ball show bank and coordination."),
    S(0, 2, "Instrument redundancy", "Why is a backup attitude indicator often powered differently from the primary?",
      ["One failure can't take both out", "It is cheaper to maintain", "It reads more accurately in turns"],
      "Using a different power source, electric instead of vacuum or a separate battery, means a single vacuum or electrical failure leaves one attitude source working."),
    S(0, 3, "Gyroscopic principles", "Which property of a gyro lets the attitude indicator hold its plane of rotation?",
      ["Rigidity in space", "Precession", "Centripetal force"],
      "A spinning rotor resists changes to its plane of rotation, which is rigidity in space. Precession is the deflection 90 degrees from an applied force and causes drift."),
]
